---
import PhaseLayout from '../../layouts/PhaseLayout.astro';
---

<PhaseLayout title="World-Class Engineer Program" activeNav="courses">

  <div class="timeline">
    <div class="tw"><div class="tw-num">CS</div><div class="tw-lbl">Core Foundation</div></div>
    <div class="tw"><div class="tw-num">DSA</div><div class="tw-lbl">Mastery</div></div>
    <div class="tw"><div class="tw-num">LLD</div><div class="tw-lbl">Low-Level Design</div></div>
    <div class="tw"><div class="tw-num">HLD</div><div class="tw-lbl">High-Level Design</div></div>
    <div class="tw"><div class="tw-num">SD</div><div class="tw-lbl">System Design</div></div>
    <div class="tw"><div class="tw-num">LOW</div><div class="tw-lbl">Low-Level Prog</div></div>
    <div class="tw"><div class="tw-num">HIGH</div><div class="tw-lbl">High-Level Prog</div></div>
    <div class="tw"><div class="tw-num">DS+AI</div><div class="tw-lbl">Distributed+ML</div></div>
    <div class="tw"><div class="tw-num">SE</div><div class="tw-lbl">Full SE Program</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="nav">
    <button class="ntab active" onclick="show(0)">CS Core</button>
    <button class="ntab" onclick="show(1)">DSA Mastery</button>
    <button class="ntab" onclick="show(2)">LLD</button>
    <button class="ntab" onclick="show(3)">HLD</button>
    <button class="ntab" onclick="show(4)">System Design</button>
    <button class="ntab" onclick="show(5)">Low-Level Prog</button>
    <button class="ntab" onclick="show(6)">High-Level Prog</button>
    <button class="ntab" onclick="show(7)">Distributed + AI/ML</button>
    <button class="ntab" onclick="show(8)">Full SE Program</button>
  </div>

  <!-- ============================================ -->
  <!-- TAB 0: CS CORE FOUNDATION                   -->
  <!-- ============================================ -->
  <div class="panel active" id="p0">
    <div class="track-header">
      <div class="track-badge badge-dsa">Months 1-6 · The bedrock of engineering</div>
      <div class="track-title">CS Core Foundation -- what separates engineers from coders</div>
      <div class="track-sub">Computer Architecture, Operating Systems, Networking, Databases, Compilers, and Distributed Systems Theory. These six pillars define the difference between someone who writes code and someone who understands machines. Every senior engineer interview, every system design round, every production incident traces back to one of these six areas. Skip them and you build on sand.</div>
    </div>

    <!-- Computer Architecture -->
    <div class="step" id="e0">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('e0')">
        <div class="step-num num-dsa">1</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Computer Architecture -- how machines actually compute</div><div class="step-meta">4-6 weeks · ~40 hours</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-e0">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-e0">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.nand2tetris.org/" target="_blank" class="res-link">Nand2Tetris (Part 1 + Part 2) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Build an entire computer from NAND gates to a working operating system. Part 1 covers hardware: logic gates, ALU, CPU, RAM, machine language. Part 2 covers software: assembler, VM translator, compiler, OS. Free on Coursera (audit) and nand2tetris.org. This single course replaces an entire semester of computer architecture because you BUILD every layer instead of just reading about it. The project-based approach means you truly understand how a CPU fetches, decodes, and executes instructions.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag must">Start here</span><span class="rtag vid">Coursera + site</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://csapp.cs.cmu.edu/" target="_blank" class="res-link">CS:APP -- Computer Systems: A Programmer's Perspective (Bryant &amp; O'Hallaron) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">The CMU textbook that every top CS program uses. Covers data representation, machine-level programs, processor architecture, memory hierarchy, linking, exceptional control flow, virtual memory, and system-level I/O. Focus chapters: Ch1 (tour), Ch3 (machine programs), Ch5 (optimization), Ch6 (memory hierarchy), Ch9 (virtual memory). The labs (bomb lab, attack lab, cache lab) are legendary -- do them.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span><span class="rtag must">CMU standard</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLhMnuBfGeCDM8pXLpqib90mDFJI-e1lpk" target="_blank" class="res-link">Stanford CS107 -- Computer Organization &amp; Systems <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Stanford's version of the systems course. Covers bits and bytes, C pointers and memory, assembly language, memory management, and system calls. The assignments are brutally effective at teaching you what the machine is actually doing under your high-level code. Available through Stanford Engineering Everywhere.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag vid">Stanford</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="do-box"><b>What you actually do:</b> Start with Nand2Tetris Part 1 (6 projects, ~3 weeks). Then read CS:APP chapters 1, 3, 5, 6 alongside the CMU labs. After this you should be able to explain exactly what happens when you type <code>int x = 42;</code> in C -- from the register allocation to the cache line fetch to the virtual memory translation. If you cannot trace that path, you are not done.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Operating Systems -->
    <div class="step" id="e1">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('e1')">
        <div class="step-num num-dsa">2</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Operating Systems Internals -- processes, memory, filesystems</div><div class="step-meta">6-8 weeks · ~50 hours</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-e1">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-e1">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/" target="_blank" class="res-link">OSTEP -- Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces (free online) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">The best OS textbook ever written, and it is completely free at pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/. Three sections: Virtualization (processes, scheduling, address spaces, paging), Concurrency (threads, locks, condition variables, semaphores), Persistence (I/O devices, file systems, journaling). The writing is exceptionally clear. Every chapter has homework simulations you can run. This book alone is a full OS course.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free online</span><span class="rtag must">The OS bible</span><span class="rtag book">Book</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://pdos.csail.mit.edu/6.828/2023/" target="_blank" class="res-link">MIT 6.S081 -- Operating System Engineering (xv6) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">MIT's legendary OS course built around xv6, a teaching Unix written in C. You implement system calls, page tables, copy-on-write fork, multithreading, a file system, and a network driver. The labs are publicly available with autograders. This is where you go from understanding OS theory to actually modifying a kernel. Lectures by Frans Kaashoek are on YouTube.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag must">Build a real kernel</span><span class="rtag vid">MIT OCW</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/@JacobSorber" target="_blank" class="res-link">Jacob Sorber -- Systems Programming (YouTube) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Short, focused videos on processes, threads, pipes, sockets, signals, memory mapping, and file I/O in C. Perfect companion to OSTEP -- when a chapter's concept feels abstract, search Jacob Sorber's channel for a 10-minute hands-on demonstration.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag vid">YouTube</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="insight"><b>Why this matters for system design interviews:</b> When you say "the process forks and the child inherits the file descriptor table," interviewers know instantly whether you actually understand what happens. OS knowledge is the difference between hand-waving at "concurrency issues" and precisely explaining race conditions, deadlocks, and the exact memory model your application runs on.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Networking -->
    <div class="step" id="e2">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('e2')">
        <div class="step-num num-dsa">3</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Networking -- TCP/IP stack, HTTP, DNS, sockets</div><div class="step-meta">4-6 weeks · ~35 hours</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-e2">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-e2">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLoCMsyE1cvdWKsLVyf6cPwCLDIZnOj0NS" target="_blank" class="res-link">Stanford CS144 -- Introduction to Computer Networking <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Stanford's networking course with publicly available labs where you build a working TCP implementation from scratch. You implement the TCP sender, receiver, and connection over an unreliable network. By the end you understand TCP not because you memorized the state diagram, but because you wrote one. The 2024 labs use modern C++.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag must">Build TCP yourself</span><span class="rtag vid">Stanford</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://beej.us/guide/bgnet/" target="_blank" class="res-link">Beej's Guide to Network Programming <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">The classic free guide to socket programming in C. Covers: socket creation, binding, listening, accepting, sending, receiving, select/poll, and building a simple HTTP server from raw sockets. Every backend engineer should have written a socket server at least once. This guide makes it approachable.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free online</span><span class="rtag must">Classic</span><span class="rtag book">Guide</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/@HusseinNasser-ku4yb" target="_blank" class="res-link">Hussein Nasser -- Backend &amp; Networking Deep Dives (YouTube) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">In-depth videos on TCP vs UDP, HTTP/1.1 vs HTTP/2 vs HTTP/3, TLS handshake, DNS resolution, connection pooling, load balancing internals, and reverse proxies. His style is perfect for engineers -- he explains the protocol layer by layer with real packet captures. Watch the TCP and HTTP/2 deep dives first.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag vid">YouTube</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="do-box"><b>The networking checkpoint:</b> Open Wireshark, capture a TCP handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK), then an HTTP request. Trace every packet through the layers: Ethernet frame, IP header, TCP segment, HTTP payload. If you can annotate every field in the packet capture, you understand networking at the level that matters for production debugging.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Databases -->
    <div class="step" id="e3">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('e3')">
        <div class="step-num num-dsa">4</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Databases -- B-trees, LSM trees, MVCC, query optimization</div><div class="step-meta">6-8 weeks · ~50 hours</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-e3">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-e3">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://dataintensive.net/" target="_blank" class="res-link">Designing Data-Intensive Applications -- Martin Kleppmann <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">DDIA is the single most important book for any software engineer working on backend systems. Part I: Data models, storage engines (B-trees vs LSM trees), encoding formats. Part II: Replication, partitioning, transactions (MVCC, serializable isolation). Part III: Derived data, batch processing, stream processing. Read cover to cover. This book will come up in every system design interview you ever do. Chapters 3 (Storage), 5 (Replication), 7 (Transactions), and 9 (Consistency) are the most critical.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">The engineering bible</span><span class="rtag book">Book</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://15445.courses.cs.cmu.edu/" target="_blank" class="res-link">CMU 15-445 -- Database Systems (Andy Pavlo) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Andy Pavlo's database course is the gold standard. All lectures on YouTube. Covers: SQL, storage engines, B+ trees, hash tables, buffer pool management, query execution, query optimization, concurrency control (2PL, MVCC), crash recovery (WAL, ARIES). The BusTub project has you build a database engine component by component. Pavlo's teaching style is electric -- he makes B-tree page splits exciting.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag must">Best DB course</span><span class="rtag vid">YouTube + CMU</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">Architecture of a Database System -- Hellerstein, Stonebraker, Hamilton (free paper)</div>
              <div class="res-desc">A survey paper that walks through the architecture of a modern RDBMS end to end: process model, parallel architecture, relational query processor, storage system, transactions, buffer management. Reading this paper gives you the aerial view that makes DDIA and Pavlo's course click together. Free PDF from UC Berkeley.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free PDF</span><span class="rtag book">Research paper</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="insight"><b>Why B-trees and LSM trees matter:</b> B-trees power every SQL database (PostgreSQL, MySQL). LSM trees power every write-heavy NoSQL store (RocksDB, Cassandra, LevelDB). Understanding their tradeoffs (read amplification vs write amplification vs space amplification) is the foundation of every storage decision you will ever make. DDIA Chapter 3 covers this perfectly.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Compilers -->
    <div class="step" id="e4">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('e4')">
        <div class="step-num num-dsa">5</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Compilers -- lexing, parsing, code generation</div><div class="step-meta">4-6 weeks · ~35 hours</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-e4">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-e4">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://craftinginterpreters.com/" target="_blank" class="res-link">Crafting Interpreters -- Robert Nystrom (free online) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">The best book on compilers/interpreters for practitioners. Free at craftinginterpreters.com. Part I builds a tree-walk interpreter in Java (jlox). Part II builds a bytecode virtual machine in C (clox). Covers: scanning, parsing (Pratt parser), ASTs, closures, garbage collection, classes, inheritance. The writing quality is extraordinary -- each chapter builds perfectly on the last. Do both implementations.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free online</span><span class="rtag must">Best compiler book</span><span class="rtag book">Book</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLDcmCgguL9rxPoVn2ykUFc8TOpLyElaED" target="_blank" class="res-link">Stanford CS143 -- Compilers (Alex Aiken) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Stanford's compiler course with full lectures on edX/YouTube. Covers lexical analysis (finite automata, regex), parsing (LL, LR), semantic analysis, code generation, and optimization. More theoretical than Crafting Interpreters -- use after you have the practical foundation from Nystrom's book.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag vid">Stanford</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="do-box"><b>Why every engineer should build a compiler once:</b> Compilers teach you parsing (used in every config file, API response, and protocol you will ever handle), tree transformations (used in every code analysis tool, linter, and transpiler), and optimization (the same pass-based approach used in ML compilers like XLA and TVM). The skills transfer everywhere.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Distributed Systems Theory -->
    <div class="step" id="e5">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('e5')">
        <div class="step-num num-dsa">6</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Distributed Systems Theory -- consensus, replication, fault tolerance</div><div class="step-meta">6-8 weeks · ~60 hours</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-e5">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-e5">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://pdos.csail.mit.edu/6.824/" target="_blank" class="res-link">MIT 6.824 -- Distributed Systems (Robert Morris) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">The most famous distributed systems course in the world. All lectures on YouTube. You read and discuss foundational papers (MapReduce, Raft, Zookeeper, Spark, Memcached at Facebook) and implement: a MapReduce framework, a Raft consensus library, a fault-tolerant key/value store, and a sharded key/value store. The labs are in Go. This course alone will put you ahead of 95% of backend engineers in distributed systems understanding.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag must">The DS course</span><span class="rtag vid">MIT</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">Essential Papers: Raft, Paxos, Dynamo, BigTable, MapReduce</div>
              <div class="res-desc">Read in this order: (1) MapReduce (Dean &amp; Ghemawat, 2004) -- the paper that started modern distributed computing. (2) Raft (Ongaro &amp; Ousterhout, 2014) -- consensus made understandable. (3) Dynamo (DeCandia et al., 2007) -- Amazon's eventually consistent key-value store. (4) BigTable (Chang et al., 2006) -- Google's distributed storage. (5) Paxos Made Simple (Lamport, 2001) -- the original consensus algorithm explained clearly. All free on Google Scholar.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag must">Read all 5</span><span class="rtag book">Research papers</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
