              <div class="res-desc">Start with the MapReduce paper (Dean &amp; Ghemawat, 2004) -- the paradigm that launched modern distributed data processing. Then read DDIA Chapter 10 which covers: Unix philosophy of data processing, MapReduce and distributed filesystems, beyond MapReduce (dataflow engines like Spark), and joins in batch processing. Understanding MapReduce deeply makes Spark, Flink, and Beam immediately comprehensible because they are all refinements of the same idea.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free paper + book</span><span class="rtag must">Foundation</span><span class="rtag book">Paper + DDIA</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.confluent.io/resources/kafka-the-definitive-guide-v2/" target="_blank" class="res-link">Kafka: The Definitive Guide (free from Confluent) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Free ebook from Confluent. Kafka is the backbone of modern event-driven architecture and real-time data pipelines. Covers: producers, consumers, consumer groups, partitions, replication, exactly-once semantics, Kafka Connect (data integration), Kafka Streams (stream processing), and Schema Registry. Understanding Kafka internals -- how log compaction works, how consumer group rebalancing works, how ISR (in-sync replicas) provide durability -- is essential for any engineer building real-time systems.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free ebook</span><span class="rtag must">Event streaming bible</span><span class="rtag book">Book</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/" target="_blank" class="res-link">Apache Spark -- Learning Spark (2nd ed.) + Official Docs <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Spark replaced MapReduce by keeping data in memory across operations. Learn: RDDs, DataFrames, Spark SQL, Structured Streaming, MLlib. The key concepts are lazy evaluation, DAG execution, shuffles, and partitioning. Learning Spark (2nd ed.) by Damji et al. is the best book. For hands-on: set up a local Spark cluster, load a 1GB dataset, write transformations, and examine the Spark UI to understand execution plans.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free docs</span><span class="rtag tool">Framework</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="do-box"><b>The streaming vs batch mental model:</b> Batch processing (MapReduce, Spark batch) = process all data, produce result. Stream processing (Kafka Streams, Flink) = process data as it arrives, continuously update result. DDIA Chapter 11 covers stream processing in depth. The real world is moving from batch to streaming for ML features (feature stores), monitoring (metrics), and analytics (real-time dashboards). Understanding both paradigms and when to use which is what separates a data engineer from a data plumber.</div>
