              <div class="res-desc">The best book on what makes a good test. Covers: the four pillars of a good test (protection against regressions, resistance to refactoring, fast feedback, maintainability), London vs Classical schools of TDD, mocks vs stubs (and why most developers overuse mocks), testing anti-patterns, and integration testing strategies. After this book you will write fewer, better tests that actually catch bugs instead of testing implementation details.</div>
