              <div class="res-desc">Google's testing chapters cover: testing overview (test sizes: small/medium/large), unit testing (best practices, avoiding brittleness), test doubles (fakes over mocks), and larger tests (integration, E2E, A/B testing). The key insight: Google uses mostly small tests (unit), fewer medium tests (integration), and very few large tests (E2E) -- the testing pyramid enforced at scale. Chapter 12 on unit testing alone is worth reading three times.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free online</span><span class="rtag must">Testing at scale</span><span class="rtag book">Book</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="do-box"><b>The testing minimum:</b> Every production system you build must have: (1) Unit tests for business logic (aim for 80% branch coverage on core logic). (2) Integration tests for API endpoints (test the contract, not the implementation). (3) One E2E test for the critical path (user signup, payment flow, core feature). (4) A CI pipeline that runs all tests on every pull request. Start with this minimum. Expand test coverage only where bugs actually appear -- data-driven testing is more effective than target-driven testing.</div>
